    "origin_id": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/jury-hits-wyoming-logistics-broker-with-9-4m-fraud-verdict",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/jury-hits-wyoming-logistics-broker-with-9-4m-fraud-verdict",
    "origin_publisher": "FreightWaves",
    "published_at": "2026-10-16T11:00:00+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-10-16T11:00:00+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-10-16T14:29:28.694561+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-10-16T14:29:28.694561+00:00",
    "body": "A California jury has awarded nearly $9.4 million to a medical supply distributor after finding that a Wyoming-based logistics broker and its managing member made fraudulent representations that led to a failed $9.5 million shipment of medical gloves.\nThe Los Angeles County Superior Court jury found Oak Sourcing & Logistics LLC and Matthew John Sandvig liable for intentional misrepresentation in a dispute with Ashtel Studios Inc., a California company that imported nitrile examination gloves during the COVID-19 pandemic.\nThe Oct. 9 verdict awarded Ashtel $9.397 million in economic damages, including $6.43 million in out-of-pocket expenditures and nearly $2.97 million in other economic losses.\nThe dispute centered on Oak Sourcing’s representations about its financial resources, customer contracts and ability to purchase millions of boxes of medical gloves.\nAccording to Ashtel’s original complaint, Sandvig told company executives in March 2021 that Oak Sourcing had an $800 million personal protective equipment contract, long-term supply commitments with hospitals and access to substantial financing.\nThose representations helped persuade Ashtel to enter into two large glove transactions totaling $14.7 million, according to the complaint.\nTwo shipments, one unpaid order\nThe companies’ first transaction, dated July 2, 2021, involved 500,000 boxes of nitrile gloves valued at $5.2 million.\nAshtel imported the gloves from China through the Port of Long Beach in 14 shipping containers. The products were subsequently transported to the company’s warehouse in Ontario, California.\nOak Sourcing eventually paid for the first shipment in August 2021, approximately three weeks after its arrival, although Ashtel alleged that certain transportation and warehousing expenses remained unpaid.\nA second purchase order, dated July 7, 2021, called for 1 million boxes of nitrile examination gloves at $9.50 per box, totaling $9.5 million.\nThe second shipment arrived in 27 containers, but Oak Sourcing allegedly failed to complete payment or take possession of the merchandise.\nAshtel alleged that the broker repeatedly delayed inspections, requested additional shipping documentation and indicated that payment would follow receipt of funds from its downstream customer.\nThe company later learned that Oak Sourcing’s purported end customer was another intermediary rather than a hospital or other direct purchaser, according to the complaint.\nAshtel said it incurred additional drayage, warehousing and storage expenses while attempting to resolve the transaction and eventually resold the gloves to reduce its losses.\nThe two purchase orders involved a combined 1.5 million boxes of gloves transported in 41 ocean shipping containers.\nJury finds intentional misrepresentation\nThe jury concluded that both Oak Sourcing and Sandvig made false representations, knew those statements were false or acted recklessly regarding their truth, and intended Ashtel to rely on them.\nJurors also found that Ashtel reasonably relied on those representations and suffered financial harm as a result.\nIn addition to the $9.397 million fraud award, the jury found that Oak Sourcing owed Ashtel $4,272,252 on an open book account claim.\nThat finding represents a separate legal claim, and the amount should not be added to the fraud award without determining how the court will address overlapping damages in its final judgment.\nThe jury also rejected Oak Sourcing’s counterclaims against Ashtel, including allegations of intentional and negligent misrepresentation, concealment and breach of the covenant of good faith and fair dealing.\nJurors further determined that Sandvig engaged in conduct involving malice, oppression or fraud, potentially exposing the defendants to punitive damages.\nHowever, Ashtel agreed to forgo punitive damages in exchange for the defendants waiving their rights to challenge the judgment through post-trial motions, appeals or other proceedings, according to Eugene Rome, founding partner of Rome LLP, which represented Ashtel.\nRome clarified that the agreement was not an additional monetary settlement.\nThe jury’s findings underscore the distinction between a failed commercial transaction and fraud, Rome said.\n“A fraud verdict is a finding that the representations about the company and the volume of business it had were knowingly false when they were made,” Rome said in an Oct. 13 statement . “That goes well beyond a breach of contract.”\nThe litigation began in March 2022, when Ashtel filed a federal lawsuit against Sandvig, Oak Sourcing and Oak Insurance Group LLC in the U.S. District Court for the Central District of California.\nThe defendants moved to dismiss the federal action for lack of jurisdiction. Ashtel subsequently voluntarily dismissed the case without prejudice, and a federal judge closed the proceeding in May 2022 without ruling on the merits.\nThe dispute continued in Los Angeles County Superior Court, where Judge Nicole M. Heeseman presided over the jury trial.\nAlthough the jury has returned its verdict, the case has not been fully resolved.\nRome told FreightWaves that Ashtel intends to submit a proposed judgment for the court’s approval.\nA separate bench trial concerning alter ego allegations involving Oak Insurance Group will also proceed at a future date, he said.\nThose allegations concern whether the related business entities should be treated as legally separate for purposes of liability. No finding against Oak Insurance Group is established by the jury verdict.\nRome said the case illustrates the risks of relying on a counterparty’s representations when entering large commercial transactions.\n“While large transactions create the prospects of substantial profits, they equally entail traps for the unwary,” Rome told FreightWaves.\nOak Sourcing & Logistics is identified in the litigation as a Wyoming-based intermediary in medical supply transactions. The available court records do not establish that it operated as a federally licensed freight broker .\nFreightWaves attorneys representing Oak Sourcing & Logistics. No responses had been received as of publication.\nWhy it matters: The verdict highlights the financial risks facing suppliers and logistics intermediaries when major transactions rely on misleading claims about customer contracts and purchasing capacity.\nMore FreightWaves articles by Noi Mahoney:\nR&R trucking CEO reports $12.7M in assets after company collapse\nMexico trucker shortages, visa restrictions squeeze cross-border capacity\nTexas trucking firm sued over age limits on drivers\nNew Jersey CDL school lawsuit questions evidence behind FMCSA shutdown\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
